{"name":"Green Column","hex":"#465149","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"column","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A solid upright structure designed usually to support a larger structure above it, such as a roof or horizontal beam, but sometimes for decoration.","A vertical line of entries in a table, usually read from top to bottom."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/column"}],"entries":[{"title":"Column","blocks":[{"text":"A column or pillar in architecture and structural engineering is a structural element that transmits, through compression, the weight of the structure above to other structural elements below. In other words, a column is a compression member. The term column applies especially to a large round support with a capital and a base or pedestal, which is made of stone, or appearing to be so. A small wooden or metal support is typically called a post. Supports with a rectangular or other non-round section are usually called piers. Throughout architectural history, especially in Classical and Rennaisance styles, the column has been central to building design."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/National_Capitol_Columns_-_Washington%2C_D.C..jpg/330px-National_Capitol_Columns_-_Washington%2C_D.C..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Column","wikidata":"Q4817"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Riksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_%282%29.jpg/500px-Riksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Riksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_%282%29.jpg/1280px-Riksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Riksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_%282%29.jpg/500px-Riksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Columns of the Parliament House in Helsinki, Finland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARiksdagen_Eduskunta_Helsingfors_(2).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Gyeongbokgung_Gyeonghoeru.jpg/500px-Gyeongbokgung_Gyeonghoeru.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Gyeongbokgung_Gyeonghoeru.jpg/500px-Gyeongbokgung_Gyeonghoeru.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"All the columns on the first floor of Gyeonghoeru in Seoul, Korea, are made of granite.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGyeongbokgung_Gyeonghoeru.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Monument_Gordon_02.JPG/500px-Monument_Gordon_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Monument_Gordon_02.JPG/1280px-Monument_Gordon_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Monument_Gordon_02.JPG/500px-Monument_Gordon_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Column of the Gordon Monument in Waterloo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMonument_Gordon_02.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/%E7%87%9F%E9%80%A0%E6%B3%95%E5%BC%8F%E5%85%AD_20.jpg/330px-%E7%87%9F%E9%80%A0%E6%B3%95%E5%BC%8F%E5%85%AD_20.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/%E7%87%9F%E9%80%A0%E6%B3%95%E5%BC%8F%E5%85%AD_20.jpg/330px-%E7%87%9F%E9%80%A0%E6%B3%95%E5%BC%8F%E5%85%AD_20.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dragon pillar from the Yingzao Fashi, Song dynasty","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E7%87%9F%E9%80%A0%E6%B3%95%E5%BC%8F%E5%85%AD_20.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/The_grammar_of_ornament_%281868%29_%2814587326250%29.jpg/250px-The_grammar_of_ornament_%28